WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:08.240 --> 00:00:14.160
Start with a right triangle that has legs of&nbsp;
length a and b and a hypotenuse of length c.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.680 --> 00:00:19.520
Rotate and shift a copy of this&nbsp;
triangle so that they align&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.520 --> 00:00:24.480
like this, and notice that the two connected&nbsp;
angles are complementary by construction.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.520 --> 00:00:33.760
We can add this line to make a trapezoid, and&nbsp;
the interior triangle here is right-angled by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.760 --> 00:00:39.680
construction. Let's compute the area of the&nbsp;
resulting trapezoid, called Garfield's trapezoid,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.680 --> 00:00:46.720
in two different ways. First of all, the&nbsp;
area of the trapezoid is the product of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.720 --> 00:00:52.560
the average of the two bases with the height.&nbsp;
So the area is a plus b over 2 times a plus b,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.560 --> 00:00:57.840
which expands to a squared&nbsp;
plus 2ab plus b squared over 2.

00:00:59.280 --> 00:01:02.960
Alternatively, the trapezoid&nbsp;
is built from three triangles,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.960 --> 00:01:11.840
so its area is the sum of the&nbsp;
areas of those three triangles.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.200 --> 00:01:20.240
The first two triangles in this sum&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.240 --> 00:01:27.440
are congruent and have the same area of&nbsp;
one-half times a times b or a times b over 2.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.520 --> 00:01:34.560
The last triangle has area one-half times c&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.560 --> 00:01:39.840
squared so that the area of the trapezoid&nbsp;
is 2 times a times b plus c squared over 2.

00:01:41.360 --> 00:01:48.800
But this means that a squared plus 2ab&nbsp;
plus b squared over 2 is equal to 2 times a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.800 --> 00:01:56.640
times b plus c squared over 2. Multiplying this&nbsp;
equality by 2 and subtracting 2 times a times b&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.640 --> 00:02:07.840
results in a squared plus b squared is equal&nbsp;
to c squared. This is the Pythagorean theorem.

